1
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:14,002
July on the Brooks River, Katmai National Park, Alaska. You are a permitted remote camera on the basalt lip of the falls.

2
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:33,056
This two-meter ledge slows millions of migrating sockeye salmon, Oncorhynchus nerka, returning from the Bering Sea to spawn.

3
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,760
Each fish leaps blind into turbulent whitewater, turning a narrow geological step into an extraordinary caloric bottleneck.

4
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,467
Straight ahead. Wait for it.

5
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:34,153
A dominant adult male brown bear, Ursus arctos, holds the prime station on the lip, intercepting salmon mid-air.

6
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:57,294
When fish numbers peak, high-graded foraging takes over: bears strip lipid-rich skin, brain, and roe first.

7
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:19,067
Pioneering stream observations by Egbert and Stokes showed how solitary brown bears form a linear dominance hierarchy here.

8
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,955
Look behind you and to your right, down into the aerated plunge pool nicknamed the jacuzzi.

9
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,307
Subordinate adults yield the lip without costly fighting and dive-fish in the turbulent eddy below.

10
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:27,368
Subtle jaw-popping, lowered ears, and posture maintain spacing only meters apart as long as salmon remain abundant.

11
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:52,495
Now look left toward the shallow slackwater margin. A maternal female keeps two cubs-of-the-year close to shore.

12
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:14,990
Field studies confirm mothers deliberately avoid prime male-dominated spots to shield cubs from adult male infanticide.

13
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,611
Years of patient observation beside their mother teach young bears which micro-site fishing technique matches their size.

14
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,926
Marine nitrogen carried inland by these salmon fertilizes Katmai's entire riparian spruce forest.

15
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:20,315
Check the waterfall live right now on the Brooks Falls brown bears camera on Digital Live Zoo.
